<!-- Head shapes: mid-sagittal vocal-tract geometries.

     A head projects the head-independent tract space (arc, offset) that
     phones live in onto 2D coordinates for rendering. Heads NEVER affect
     distance: phone identity does not depend on whose head you imagine,
     and the shipped confusion matrix must stay reproducible.

     Each head declares its midline as control points (arc, x, y) in a
     normalized box - lips at the left, glottis at the lower right, the
     palate above the oral run and the pharyngeal bend behind - plus the
     tract diameter along the arc, which scales how far an offset carries
     a point from the midline toward the wall. Adult heads additionally
     declare the measured hard-palate roof as an absolute outline. This is
     deliberately separate from aperture: the roof apex is near arc 0.30,
     while the greatest tongue-to-palate clearance is near arc 0.24.

     Each head also declares its rest posture: where the articulators sit
     when not speaking - jaw and lips closed, tongue neutral, velum
     lowered for nasal breathing. Rest is RENDERING geometry, not
     features: silence is featurally null (no articulation, distance 1.0
     from every speech sound), but it still has to be drawn somewhere, and
     an utterance starts and ends at rest, which gives animations their
     home position.

     Geometries are schematic, hand-placed from published mid-sagittal
     anatomy; proportions follow the usual findings (a child's tract is
     shorter overall and proportionally shorter in the pharynx; an adult
     female tract is shorter than an adult male's, again more so in the
     pharynx). They are not measurements, with one exception: the adult
     midline `diameter` over arc 0.11-0.40 is now the shape measured from
     the X-Ray Microbeam database (Westbury 1994) over all 48 speakers.
     Each adult point below says whether it is measured or extrapolated.
     Diameters elsewhere, all x/y coordinates, and the whole child head are
     unchanged and remain hand-placed. See docs/articulatory-data.md.

     The measured roof reaches its front edge at arc 0.11 — the palate
     outline's own median front edge — where the roof drops to the alveolar
     ridge, the front-low region every speaker's PAL.DAT shows, rising into
     the vault. The front is carried at arc 0.11, 0.13, 0.15, 0.19 and 0.24,
     while the separately declared roof carries the outline itself through
     the vault apex at arc 0.30. Each diameter is the measured
     palate-roof height carried into
     the model's aperture: the roof's drop from the arc-0.24 peak, scaled so
     the peak diameter equals the per-sex peak clearance (30.5 mm male,
     27.25 mm female), with the floor held at the vault level forward of the
     peak. The shape is aggregated over PAL.DAT itself (46 speakers, JW48 and
     JW63 dropped), which the tongue cannot reach: the tongue rarely touches
     the low ridge, so the clearance profile reads there biased narrow and
     cannot ground the front; the palate outline is a static cast and
     measures the roof directly.

     The arc 0.11, 0.15, 0.24 and 0.40 points were inserted to carry the
     measured shape; their x and y are linear interpolations of the midline
     that was already there, so the drawn centerline is exactly what it was
     and only `diameter` moved. Arc 0.11, 0.15 and 0.40 name no phonetic
     place, so they are stated in scripts/invariants.py UNDECLARED_VERTEX_ARCS
     with that reason; 0.13, 0.19 and 0.24 are the declared alveolar,
     postalveolar and alveolo-palatal.

     Behind the measured run the diameters keep their original *shape* but are
     rescaled to join it — each by the ratio the seam moved (0.13/0.18 for the
     adult male, 0.12/0.16 for the female). Those points were hand-placed to sit
     under an oral run of 0.18 that measurement has since put at 0.13, and left
     unscaled they produced the steepest slope in the whole profile, a flare
     that was an artifact of joining two differently-derived numbers. Rescaling
     removes it. The cost is that the profile now falls monotonically behind the
     peak, so it represents no oropharyngeal narrowing; if one is wanted there,
     it needs a source, because this one has none. Heads still NEVER affect distance; verified by a
     full sweep over all 8060 units and 9591 pairs, which moved nothing. -->
<!-- Orientation: the head faces left. x runs from the lips at 0 to the
     glottis at 1, so front articulations sit on the left and back ones on the
     right. That matches how the IPA vowel chart is drawn — front vowels left,
     back vowels right — so a tract figure and a vowel chart can be read side
     by side without one of them being mirrored. -->
<heads default="adult-male">

  <head name="adult-male" desc="Adult male mid-sagittal tract" length-cm="17.5">
    <rest arc="0.38" offset="0.18" tip-arc="0.11" tip-offset="1.0" lips="closed" jaw="closed" velum="lowered"/>
    <midline>
      <point arc="0.00" x="0.06" y="0.62" diameter="0.16" provenance="extrapolated"/>       <!-- extrapolated: the lip aperture; PAL.DAT does not reach the lips -->
      <point arc="0.11" x="0.1954" y="0.6538" diameter="0.098" provenance="measured"/>  <!-- measured: the palate outline's front edge, the alveolar ridge -->
      <point arc="0.13" x="0.22" y="0.66" diameter="0.1105" provenance="measured"/>    <!-- measured: rising off the alveolar ridge -->
      <point arc="0.15" x="0.2389" y="0.6621" diameter="0.126" provenance="measured"/> <!-- measured: the steep front flank of the arch -->
      <point arc="0.17" x="0.2579" y="0.6642" diameter="0.1436" provenance="measured"/><!-- measured: mid-flank, the steepest rise -->
      <point arc="0.19" x="0.2768" y="0.6663" diameter="0.1575" provenance="measured"/> <!-- measured: the flank easing toward the vault -->
      <point arc="0.21" x="0.2958" y="0.6684" diameter="0.1716" provenance="measured"/> <!-- measured: the vault shoulder, where the dome rounds over -->
      <point arc="0.24" x="0.3242" y="0.6716" diameter="0.18" provenance="measured"/>   <!-- measured: the vault peak, rounded, over arc 0.22 to 0.28 -->
      <point arc="0.32" x="0.40" y="0.68" diameter="0.16" provenance="measured"/>       <!-- measured: 0.90 of the peak -->
      <point arc="0.40" x="0.4738" y="0.6677" diameter="0.13" provenance="measured"/>   <!-- measured: 0.73 of the peak -->
      <point arc="0.45" x="0.52" y="0.66" diameter="0.13" provenance="extrapolated"/>       <!-- extrapolated: held flat past the palate trace -->
      <point arc="0.56" x="0.61" y="0.61" diameter="0.123" provenance="extrapolated"/>      <!-- extrapolated: original shape, rescaled to join the measured run -->
      <point arc="0.74" x="0.70" y="0.44" diameter="0.108" provenance="extrapolated"/>      <!-- extrapolated: same -->
      <point arc="0.87" x="0.73" y="0.30" diameter="0.094" provenance="extrapolated"/>      <!-- extrapolated: same -->
      <point arc="1.00" x="0.75" y="0.16" diameter="0.079" provenance="extrapolated"/>      <!-- extrapolated: same -->
